  "text": "The last four are quicker, and three of them are about the inputs rather than the model. A discriminative model learns the probability of the label given the input, like logistic regression. A generative model learns how the inputs are spread within each class, like naive Bayes, then classifies with Bayes' theorem. It can generate data, and it copes better with few labels. Scale features for anything built on distances or gradient descent: K N N, k-means, support vector machines and linear models. On the wine data, K N N goes from sixty-nine percent to ninety-five once the features are standardized. A tree scores eighty-nine either way. One-hot encoding gives each category its own column of zeros and ones. Ordinal encoding maps ordered categories to integers. Target encoding uses each category's mean outcome, which leaks unless it's computed inside cross-validation. And feature selection comes in three kinds. Filters score each feature on its own. Wrappers, like recursive feature elimination, try subsets with a model. Embedded methods select while training, like the lasso. Here, recursive elimination keeps five of the thirty tumor features. Every model here is built from parts you can point to: a line, a cut, a distance. A neural network learns its own. Next: deep learning.",
